## Why This Is Asked
Streaming data often requires maintaining a running statistic under insertions and deletions. The median is robust and a common interview target for dynamic data structures.

## Key Concepts
- Two-heap approach: keep lower half in a max-heap and upper half in a min-heap.
- Duplicates supported; lazy deletion via a delay map.
- Rebalancing invariant: size difference at most 1; median derived from heap tops.

## Code Example
```javascript
class MedianDS {
  constructor(){
    this.lo = new MaxHeap(); // lower half
    this.hi = new MinHeap(); // upper half
    this.delayed = new Map();
    this.sizeLo = 0;
    this.sizeHi = 0;
  }
  _cleanTop(h) {
    const top = h.peek();
    while (top !== null && this.delayed.get(top) > 0) {
      h.pop();
      this.delayed.set(top, this.delayed.get(top) - 1);
    }
  }
  add(x){
    if (this.sizeLo === 0 || x <= this.lo.peek()) {
      this.lo.push(x); this.sizeLo++;
    } else { this.hi.push(x); this.sizeHi++; }
    this._balance();
  }
  remove(x){
    const loTop = this.lo.peek();
    if (loTop !== null && x <= loTop) {
      this.delayed.set(x, (this.delayed.get(x) || 0) + 1);
      this.sizeLo--;
      if (x === loTop) this._cleanTop(this.lo);
    } else {
      this.delayed.set(x, (this.delayed.get(x) || 0) + 1);
      this.sizeHi--;
      this._cleanTop(this.hi);
    }
    this._balance();
  }
  _balance(){
    if (this.sizeLo > this.sizeHi + 1) {
      this.hi.push(this.lo.pop()); this.sizeLo--; this.sizeHi++;
    } else if (this.sizeHi > this.sizeLo) {
      this.lo.push(this.hi.pop()); this.sizeHi--; this.sizeLo++;
    }
    this._cleanTop(this.lo); this._cleanTop(this.hi);
  }
  getMedian(){
    if (this.sizeLo === 0) return null;
    if (this.sizeLo > this.sizeHi) return this.lo.peek();
    return (this.lo.peek() + this.hi.peek()) / 2;
  }
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem evaluates proficiency in interval stabbing, weighted multisets, and persistent versioning within a single challenge, while also testing the ability to handle overlapping intervals and duplicates elegantly.

## Key Concepts
- **Segment tree over coordinates** for efficient interval partitioning with O(log n) node coverage per operation
- **Per-node multisets** implemented as order-statistic trees enabling duplicate-aware weight tracking and rank queries
- **Path-copying persistence** to maintain an immutable version history with O(log n) space overhead per update
- **Multiset semantics** for handling duplicate intervals through count-based insertions and deletions

## Code Example
```javascript
class IntervalMultiset {
  constructor() {
    this.roots = new Map(); // version -> segment tree root
    this.roots.set(0, null);
    this.currentVersion = 0;
  }

  insertInterval(l, r, w) {
    this.currentVersion++;
    const newRoot = insert(this.roots.get(this.currentVersion - 1), l, r, w);
    this.roots.set(this.currentVersion, newRoot);
  }

  eraseInterval(l, r, w) {
    this.currentVersion++;
    const newRoot = erase(this.roots.get(this.currentVersion - 1), l, r, w);
    this.roots.set(this.currentVersion, newRoot);
  }

  sumWeightsCoveringPoint(p, t) {
    return querySum(this.roots.get(t), p);
  }

  kthLargestWeightCoveringPoint(p, t, k) {
    return queryKth(this.roots.get(t), p, k);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| insertInterval | O(log² n) | O(log n) new nodes |
| eraseInterval | O(log² n) | O(log n) new nodes |
| sumWeightsCoveringPoint | O(log n) | O(1) |
| kthLargestWeightCoveringPoint | O(log² n) | O(1) |

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates practical data-structure composition: a queue with constant-time min tracking, a common interview pattern for streams and real-time dashboards.

## Key Concepts

- Two-stack queue pattern
- Per-stack min tracking
- Amortized analysis and edge-case handling for duplicates

## Code Example

```javascript
class MinQueue {
  constructor(){ this.inStack = []; this.outStack = []; }
  enqueue(x){ const minIn = this.inStack.length ? Math.min(x, this.inStack[this.inStack.length-1][1]) : x; this.inStack.push([x, minIn]); }
  dequeue(){ if (!this.outStack.length){ while (this.inStack.length){ const [val] = this.inStack.pop(); const minOut = this.outStack.length ? Math.min(val, this.outStack[this.outStack.length-1][1]) : val; this.outStack.push([val, minOut]); } }
    const popped = this.outStack.pop(); return popped ? popped[0] : undefined; }
  getMin(){ const minIn = this.inStack.length ? this.inStack[this.inStack.length-1][1] : Infinity; const minOut = this.outStack.length ? this.outStack[this.outStack.length-1][1] : Infinity; return Math.min(minIn, minOut); }
}
```

